Upgrading
*********


Upgrading to Version 4.0
========================

Permissions are now decided by [voters |authorization]. The `Nette\Security\Authorizator` interface stays as the basis of the [ACL |acl], but the `User` object no longer works with it directly:

- the methods `User::setAuthorizator()`, `getAuthorizator()` and `getAuthorizatorIfExists()` are deprecated; a custom authorizator registered as a service keeps working, you just get a notice
- the third parameter of the `User` constructor is now a list of voters; `Permission` is one of them, so `new User($storage, $authenticator, $acl)` keeps working, but you cannot pass a custom authorizator there
- `User::isAllowed()` accepts the operation only as a string or `null`
- as a voter, the ACL doesn't mind an unknown role or resource: `$user->isAllowed('nonexistent')` returns `false` instead of throwing
- a `deny()` rule in the ACL overrides the other voters too: what the ACL explicitly forbids, no other voter can allow
- roles are strings only: an identity returning a `Nette\Security\Role` object throws `InvalidStateException`, `Permission::isAllowed()` accepts the role only as a string, and `getQueriedRole()` and `getQueriedResource()` return names; the `Role` interface is deprecated, `Resource` stays
- the `security.authorizator` service is of type `Nette\Security\Permission`

Rewriting a custom authorizator as a voter is easy. Instead of a role you get a [principal |authorization#Principal: Who the Decision Is About] and ask about roles with its `isInRole()` method:

```php
// before
class MyAuthorizator implements Nette\Security\Authorizator
{
	public function isAllowed($role, $resource, $privilege): bool
	{
		return $role === 'admin' || ($role === 'registered' && $resource === 'article');
	}
}

// now
class MyVoter implements Nette\Security\Voter
{
	public function vote(Principal $principal, mixed $resource, ?string $privilege): Vote
	{
		return Vote::allowIf(
			$principal->isInRole('admin')
			|| ($principal->isInRole('registered') && $resource === 'article'),
		);
	}
}
```

An ACL assertion that decided by role and resource objects is rewritten as a voter. It gets the article and the principal directly, so it no longer needs a role object:

```php
// before
$acl->allow('registered', 'article', 'edit', function (Permission $acl): bool {
	return $acl->getQueriedRole()->id === $acl->getQueriedResource()->authorId;
});
$acl->isAllowed(new Registered($userId), $article, 'edit');

// now
class ArticleVoter implements Nette\Security\Voter
{
	public function vote(Principal $principal, mixed $resource, ?string $privilege): Vote
	{
		return Vote::allowIf(
			$resource instanceof Article
			&& $privilege === 'edit'
			&& $principal->isInRole('registered')
			&& $principal->hasId($resource->authorId),
		);
	}
}

$user->isAllowed($article, 'edit');
```


Upgrading to Version 3.1
========================

- the `Nette\Security\Authenticator` interface differs from `IAuthenticator` in the signature of the `authenticate(string $user, string $password)` method
- the `Nette\Security\UserStorage` interface differs from `Nette\Security\IUserStorage`
- the class `Nette\Security\Identity` was renamed to `Nette\Security\SimpleIdentity` so that in the distant future the interface `Nette\Security\IIdentity` can be renamed to `Identity`
- the interface `Nette\Security\UserStorage`, which decides where the information about the logged-in user is kept, is brand new and differs from the original `Nette\Security\IUserStorage`; it is what `User::getStorage()` returns. Implementations storing the data in a session or in a cookie are available.
- an authenticator can implement the interface `Nette\Security\IdentityHandler` and thus influence what happens to the identity when it is saved to and restored from the storage, for example to refresh the roles

How to use `IdentityHandler` and how to build a website with logging in that needs no sessions at all is shown in the article [News in Nette Security 3.1 |https://blog.nette.org/en/news-in-nette-security-3-1].


Upgrading to Version 3.0
========================

- the `Nette\Security\Passwords` class is now used as an object, i.e. the methods are no longer static
